1. 红(2) 物体向左滑向红(2) 墙壁列：按距左墙壁的距离从近到远依次处理。每个物体整体水平左移，直到物体的最左像素紧邻墙壁列或紧邻已停靠的红色物体的右边缘。清除物体原位置。 2. 蓝(1) 物体向右滑向蓝(1) 墙壁列：按距右墙壁的距离从近到远依次处理。每个物体整体水平右移，直到物体的最右像素紧邻墙壁列或紧邻已停靠的蓝色物体的左边缘。清除物体原位置。 3. 绿(3) 物体不移动，直接擦除（置为背景色）。 4. 统计滑动后网格中红(2) 的总像素数（包括墙壁列的像素），记为 Pr；蓝(1) 的总像素数（包括墙壁列的像素），记为 Pb；绿(3) 的原始总像素数，记为 Pg。 5. 创建新的输出网格。分别构建三个实心正方形：红色正方形边长 sqrt(Pr)，蓝色正方形边长 sqrt(Pb)，绿色正方形边长 sqrt(Pg)。 6. 将三个正方形按面积从小到大从左到右排列，底边对齐，相邻正方形之间留一列背景色间隔。输出网格高度为最大正方形的边长，宽度为三个正方形宽度加两列间隔之和。  **关键洞察**：墙壁列的像素也计入颜色总数——滑动不仅改变物体位置，还通过"合并到墙壁"增大了红/蓝的有效像素数。观察者必须注意墙壁像素是否计入，这需要对比多个示例中墙壁列长度与输出正方形边长的关系才能确认。绿色物体虽不滑动也不出现在中间结果中，但其像素数仍参与最终排列。  --- </new_puzzle>  ## 机制相似参考题  下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：优先使用新题中已经写明的原语、组合逻辑、来源原语和显式参考题 id；再用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。  已选参考：eval/21897d95, train/f0100645, train/465b7d93, train/337b420f, train/df978a02, eval/a251c730, train/54dc2872, train/e9fc42f2  新题正文显式提及的参考题 id：1b8318e3, 20fb2937, 21897d95, 337b420f, 465b7d93, 50c07299, 54dc2872, 60d73be6, 758abdf0, 83eb0a57, 84ba50d3, 8abad3cf, 984d8a3e, 9caf5b84, a251c730, abc82100, df978a02, e9fc42f2, f0100645, f931b4a8  文件头参考题组 id（弱提示）：none  <reference_puzzle rank="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="21897d95"> <similarity> score=1.0925 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0125 code_feature=0.7500 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/21897d95.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/21897d95.json </paths> <description> Repaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.  Input structure: - The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions. - Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries. - Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color. - An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.  Rule: - For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint. - The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color. - For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement. - All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region. - A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.  Key insight: - The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "monochrome regions with T-shaped color-1 control markers",   "transform": "recolor the region pointed to by each T stem using the original color from the two diagonal source cells behind the stem; colored-center T markers use the center color instead; erase marker cells into the source-side region",   "grid_shape": "same, except rotate 90 degrees when indicated by the colored-center control orientation",   "notes": "small non-rectangular colored scraps are absorbed into the neighboring region" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="2" source="train" puzzle_id="f0100645"> <similarity> score=1.0702 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0083 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f0100645.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f0100645.json </paths> <description> Slide each inner colored object horizontally into the same-colored vertical wall until it touches an obstacle.  Input structure - The background is the dominant color. - The only foreground colors are two non-background colors. - One foreground color fills the left column and the other fills the right column. - Extra 8-connected objects of each wall color appear away from that color's wall.  Rule - Clear the extra objects from their original positions. - For each wall color, process its 8-connected extra objects from nearest to farthest from the matching wall. - Slide each object horizontally toward its matching wall as far as possible without overlapping the wall or an already settled object of the same color. - Keep the two vertical wall columns fixed.  Key insight - The side columns act like color-specific gravity walls: same-colored objects compact sideways against their own wall while preserving each object's shape. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_along_path",   "src": "8-connected objects away from the full-height side wall of the same color",   "path": "horizontal slide toward the matching side wall, stopping just before the wall or an already settled same-color object would overlap",   "trajectory": "translate each object rigidly to its final unobstructed position",   "transform": "clear original object positions, keep both side walls fixed, and preserve each moved object's color and shape",   "multi_step": "For each wall color, settle objects from nearest to farthest from that wall so later objects can stop against earlier same-color objects." } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="3" source="train" puzzle_id="465b7d93"> <similarity> score=1.0646 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0228 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/465b7d93.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/465b7d93.json </paths> <description> Move the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.  Input structure: - The background is the dominant color. - There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular   one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it. - The frame interior is initially background.  Rule: - Clear the original sprite from its outside location. - Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to   interior extremes. - Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of   adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside   those drawn segments.  Key insight: The outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior, not a bitmap to scale by block replication. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "small colored sprite s outside the frame",   "dst": "hollow rectangular frame interior d",   "match_cond": "unique s maps to unique d; s.bbox extremes align to d.bbox extremes",   "transform": "clear s; stretch s into d by mapping bbox extremes, drawing 4-adjacent segments, and filling enclosed regions" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="4" source="train" puzzle_id="337b420f"> <similarity> score=1.0550 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0179 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/337b420f.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/337b420f.json </paths> <description> Keep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.  Input structure: - The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns. - Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.  Rule: - In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite. - Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas. - If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.  Key insight: - The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_in_order",   "src": "largest 4-connected foreground object in each 5x5 panel",   "dst": "overlay onto one 5x5 canvas in left-t